        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "57fea5ad-308f-5ca6-95ac-aea816eec132",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Travis McGee, John D. MacDonald, Janet Evanovich, Stephanie Plum, Rod Taylor, Steffie Hall\n- Works: Travis McGee book series, Stephanie Plum series, Darker than Amber\n- Concepts: title theme in detective and mystery fiction, bestseller lists\n\nFacts:\n- John D. MacDonald created Travis McGee as a fictional character.\n- The 21 Travis McGee books use color-themed titles and are an early example of a detective or mystery fiction series with a title theme.\n- Janet Evanovich began her career by writing short contemporary romance novels under the pen name Steffie Hall.\n- Janet Evanovich became famous for writing contemporary mystery novels featuring Stephanie Plum, a former lingerie buyer from Trenton, New Jersey, who becomes a bounty hunter after losing her job.\n- The Stephanie Plum series appeared on The New York Times, USA Today, The Wall Street Journal, and Amazon bestseller lists.\n- Rod Taylor starred as Travis McGee in the film Darker than Amber.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b7030dca-170c-5d78-ae1f-5171817b48a2",
        "text": "Page: Travis McGee. Sentence: Travis McGee is a fictional character, created by American mystery writer John D. MacDonald.\nPage: Travis McGee. Sentence: All 21 books have the theme of a color in the title, one of the earliest examples of detective/mystery fiction series to have a 'title theme' (e.g. the Sue Grafton 'alphabet' series; Janet Evanovich's 'number' series of Stephanie Plum books, etc.)\nPage: Janet Evanovich. Sentence: She began her career writing short contemporary romance novels under the pen name Steffie Hall, but gained fame authoring a series of contemporary mysteries featuring Stephanie Plum, a former lingerie buyer from Trenton, New Jersey, who becomes a bounty hunter to make ends meet after losing her job.\nPage: Janet Evanovich. Sentence: The novels in this series have been on \"The New York Times\", USA Today, \"Wall Street Journal\" and Amazon bestseller lists.\nPage: Darker than Amber (film). Sentence: It starred Rod Taylor as Travis McGee.",
